handling, and value semantics are the big shifts. Read 03–05 (types, functions), 07 (slices — they're not Python lists), 15 (errors — no exceptions), and 16–20 (concurrency — goroutines\u002Fchannels are unique to Go).",[87,756,758],{"id":757},"if-youre-coming-from-ccrust","If you're coming from C\u002FC++\u002FRust",[15,760,761],{},"Go is simpler but has surprises: garbage collection (no manual memory management), goroutines (not OS threads), channels (not mutexes as default), and implicit interfaces. Read 10 (escape analysis), 12 (interfaces), 16–18 (concurrency), 27 (profiling — escape analysis is Go's answer to zero-cost abstractions).",[87,763,765],{"id":764},"if-youre-a-senior-engineer","If you're a senior engineer",[15,767,768],{},"Start with 01 (toolchain internals), 10 (escape analysis), 12 (interface nil trap), 15 (error wrapping chains), 16 (G-M-P scheduler), 20 (context cancellation trees), 26 (concurrency patterns), 27 (profiling). The exercises in 28 are production-graded.",[19,770,772],{"id":771},"what-makes-this-course-different","What Makes This Course Different",[63,774,775,781,787,793,799],{},[27,776,777,780],{},[30,778,779],{},"Code-first",": every concept is demonstrated with production-grade annotated code, not prose.",[27,782,783,786],{},[30,784,785],{},"Anti-patterns → fixes",": every chapter shows the wrong way, explains the trap, and gives the correct production pattern.",[27,788,789,792],{},[30,790,791],{},"Edge cases and gotchas",": each chapter includes subtle boundary conditions, compiler quirks, and runtime failure modes.",[27,794,795,798],{},[30,796,797],{},"Quick quizzes",": test understanding with tricky code snippets at the end of each chapter.",[27,800,801,804,805,807],{},[30,802,803],{},"Production focus",": Docker multi-stage builds, graceful shutdown, supply chain (",[69,806,582],{},"), race detection, profiling — not just syntax.",[19,809,811],{"id":810},"prerequisites-for-chapters","Prerequisites for Chapters",[92,813,814,824